// crates/nikaia-std/src/text.nika
//
// `std::text`, written in Nikaia.
//
// This file is compiled to Rust by the Stage 0 compiler when `nikaia-std` is
// built (`build.rs`), and the result is what `nikaia_std::text` is. It is the
// first piece of Nikaia's own standard library that is Nikaia rather than Rust,
// and it is small on purpose: what a Nikaia file can be here is exactly what
// Stage 0 can lower, and the moment that grows, so can this (ADR-014).

// The value of a decimal digit: '7' is 7.
//
// A free function rather than a method on the character: Rust's own
// `char::to_digit` has this name with a different shape, an inherent method
// wins over anything a library adds, and a call that resolves to the wrong
// thing is worse than a name that reads differently (ADR-013 D1).
// `sync` (Part II, 12.1): pure computation, it cannot pause and does no I/O.
// Saying so is what puts it in the ledger, and the ledger is what lets a
// `sync` caller in another program use it (Part III, 13.5).
pub fn digit_value(c: char) -> i32 sync {
    return c as i32 - 48
}
